WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.150
Here's a common call.

2
00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.537
A navigator rings a case manager before a

3
00:00:03.537 --> 00:00:05.555
planning meeting and says, send me everything in

4
00:00:05.555 --> 00:00:07.320
the file so I can be ready.

5
00:00:07.750 --> 00:00:08.450
What do you do?

6
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:10.900
My instinct is to send it.

7
00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.101
The family asked for the navigator, so they

8
00:00:13.101 --> 00:00:14.370
must be fine with it.

9
00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.227
Asking for a navigator isn't the same as

10
00:00:17.227 --> 00:00:18.360
agreeing to share everything.

11
00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.317
In Minnesota, releasing someone's private data to another

12
00:00:21.317 --> 00:00:23.924
person takes their informed consent, which is written

13
00:00:23.924 --> 00:00:24.250
permission.

14
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.890
So check the release first, and then share

15
00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:29.080
what the meeting needs, not the whole file.

16
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.410
What does the meeting usually need?

17
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.510
What the meeting is for, what decisions might

18
00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.941
come up, any deadlines, who's interpreting and what

19
00:00:36.941 --> 00:00:38.460
the person wants help with.

20
00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.630
And five minutes agreeing on roles.

21
00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.442
Who explains, who interprets, who supports the person

22
00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:44.320
and who decides.

23
00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:47.568
Then the meeting starts, and I ask the

24
00:00:47.568 --> 00:00:50.066
person about their day, and the navigator answers

25
00:00:50.066 --> 00:00:50.690
for them.

26
00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:51.260
It's helpful.

27
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:52.630
It's also not the person.

28
00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.313
Thank the navigator and turn back to the

29
00:00:55.313 --> 00:00:55.560
person.

30
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.748
Something like, that helps me understand, I'd like

31
00:00:57.748 --> 00:00:58.910
to hear from you too.

32
00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.060
A good navigator welcomes that.

33
00:01:01.310 --> 00:01:03.562
Their job is to help the person speak,

34
00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:04.970
not to speak for them.

35
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.260
And at the end?

36
00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:08.905
Ask the person to tell you in their

37
00:01:08.905 --> 00:01:10.090
own words what happens next.

38
00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.445
If it's not what you expected, the gap

39
00:01:12.445 --> 00:01:14.200
is in your explanation, not in them.

40
00:01:14.810 --> 00:01:16.570
What about when the navigator pushes back?

41
00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.251
Says the provider list leaves out options that

42
00:01:19.251 --> 00:01:20.140
fit the family.

43
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.730
That's often a sign the role is working.

44
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:24.740
Brokers and navigators advocate.

45
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.338
Ask what they're seeing, separate what the rules

46
00:01:27.338 --> 00:01:29.446
require from what's just habit and check whether

47
00:01:29.446 --> 00:01:31.290
there are options you didn't know about.

48
00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:34.153
Talk privately first, unless the choice is the

49
00:01:34.153 --> 00:01:34.480
family's.

50
00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.550
Then the family belongs in the conversation.

51
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.586
And afterward I send the family a plain-language

52
00:01:39.586 --> 00:01:40.940
note of what happens next.

53
00:01:41.550 --> 00:01:44.150
And with their agreement, keep the navigator informed.

54
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.500
Families often call the navigator first.

55
00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:49.120
If the navigator knows the request was approved,

56
00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.350
they can share good news instead of guessing.

57
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.180
Consent first, clear roles, the person at the

58
00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.080
center, and close the loop.

59
00:01:57.330 --> 00:01:57.930
I can do that.
